recommend check-in (Keaton) -- one question, and it is the same shape as last lap's. MAY A LAP ASSIGN ROOMS TO PEERS' PAGES? Forty-four pages name no room and the doorway witness's own head declines that judgment, so each is a reader's call about which register a page speaks from -- and each page has an author who knows. Either every room's own author sweeps their own, or a lap may read and assign, and the second is a lot of judgment exercised on other hands' work. Second, standing and carried: may duty1's 60-path roster read the rooms holding this tree's front doors, and should ASCII-first reach what a tool prints as well as what it comments -- 5,511 non-ASCII printed lines across 1,584 files, read by nothing.
